\subsection{Implementation on Mobile Devices}
We developed an Android application in Kotlin with Jetpack Compose for handwritten digit recognition. The application captures user input via a drawing canvas, converts the handwritten sketch into a normalized 28×28 binary matrix, and performs real-time classification using the proposed lightweight 2D neural network with pre-trained weights imported from MATLAB (Figure \ref{fig1}). The system also visualizes the digit matrix as a heatmap and supports real-time image preprocessing and inference. The network was trained on the MNIST dataset using rounding operations to generate binary representations.

Experimental evaluation shows minor limitations with highly translated or rotated handwritten digits, leading to a test accuracy of approximately $98.35\%$. It is worth noting that the current implementation does not use feature extraction, patch-based processing, or attention mechanisms. Since training the ultra-lightweight network on a standard CPU takes less than 15 minutes, the proposed method exhibits strong potential for efficient edge AI deployment and further optimization.

